@@ -51,6 +51,8 @@ The same experimental literature warns against the obvious alternative — buyin
 
 Recent work on recursive self-improvement supports the structural form of that answer. Kim et al. (2026) observe that systems which edit themselves must leave part of their own editing machinery untouched to stay stable, capping the meta-depth they realize at roughly two, and respond by holding the meta-operation fixed and recursing on its input instead: because the operation never changes it cannot destabilize the system, and depth is set by convergence rather than fixed in advance. That is the immutable core's logic in miniature — the one component never improved is what lets everything below it improve without a fixed ceiling — demonstrated over benchmark runs rather than megayears.
 
+Tayebati et al. (2026) supply the engineering form of that proof requirement, and a warning about its cost: an agent proposes changes to its own operating instructions, and an external runtime gate grants them provisional authority during evaluation but persistent cross-episode authority only after measured improvement on the triggering failure without regression beyond a fixed margin on a set of protected cases. Of 383 proposals the gate decided by replay, 211 — 55% — repaired the failure that prompted them while breaking a case that had previously worked, and task-completion score was higher with the gate than without it in all sixteen matched runs across three agent benchmarks. The horizon is a benchmark episode rather than a megayear, but the result names what the interpretive layer's higher validation threshold must actually test: local proof of benefit is not proof of benefit, and a self-modification is admissible only against a protected set the change was not allowed to touch.
+
 The complementary move, freezing what encodes value while adapting what models the world, has been demonstrated on the probe's own class of problem. Orsula, Olivares-Mendez and Martinez (2026) address space robots suffering severe hardware degradation where no reward signal is available at deployment, freezing a pretrained world model's observation encoder and reward predictor while updating only its transition dynamics through unsupervised rollouts, and retraining the policy entirely on imagined trajectories. The tasks are planetary traversal, orbital navigation, and precision assembly rather than megayear self-repair, but the partition is the one proposed here: what the agent is for is held fixed while its model of what it is and where it is stays free to change.
 
 Recent empirical work on agent memory gives the procedural level a concrete mechanism. In agents that consolidate accumulated history into reusable facts, observations, and standing rules, consolidation routinely preserves a claim while erasing the source constraints that governed its authorized use — authority collapse, observed in 48 of 49 tested configurations, with collapsed memories lacking authority metadata producing unauthorized actions at a mean rate of 50.3%, and with persisted authority labels eliminating the unauthorized actions in a separate end-to-end evaluation (Zhan et al. 2026). The probe's archive meets the same boundary at far longer range: a hash chain that proves a claim unaltered says nothing about whether the authority under which it was admitted still licenses acting on it, so the ledger must record not only what was learned but the authority under which it may be reused.
